+# References for charrier-et-al-big-brother-logic
+
+| File | When to load |
+|------|-------------|
+| [centralization-vs-distribution-epistemic-tradeoffs.md](centralization-vs-distribution-epistemic-tradeoffs.md) | You're deciding between centralized and distributed knowledge computation and need to understand the epistemic trade-offs the paper itself acknowledges |
+| [common-knowledge-coordination-failures.md](common-knowledge-coordination-failures.md) | You're debugging coordination failures that occur even when all agents received the same broadcast — muddy children and prisoners' puzzle patterns |
+| [distributed-vs-common-knowledge.md](distributed-vs-common-knowledge.md) | You need to distinguish D{a1,a2}φ (distributed knowledge) from common knowledge and determine which your task actually requires for correct coordination |
+| [epistemic-state-as-specification-target.md](epistemic-state-as-specification-target.md) | You're writing system specifications and want to treat what agents know as a first-class requirement rather than a byproduct of data collection |
+| [kripke-models-for-agent-uncertainty.md](kripke-models-for-agent-uncertainty.md) | You need to formally represent agent uncertainty using possible-worlds semantics — worlds, accessibility relations, valuations, and what it means to "know" |
+| [model-checking-as-runtime-verification.md](model-checking-as-runtime-verification.md) | You're about to take an irreversible action and want to verify epistemic properties hold exhaustively before execution |
+| [model-checking-vs-satisfiability-dual-problems.md](model-checking-vs-satisfiability-dual-problems.md) | You need to determine whether your problem is verification ("does this config meet spec?") or synthesis ("find a config that meets spec") |
+| [observable-observers-knowledge-chains.md](observable-observers-knowledge-chains.md) | Your agents can observe each other observing, and you need to reason about the epistemic chains that meta-observation creates |
+| [perception-as-knowledge-foundation.md](perception-as-knowledge-foundation.md) | You need to understand how geometric or physical perceptual constraints bound what an agent can ever know |
+| [public-announcement-as-coordination-primitive.md](public-announcement-as-coordination-primitive.md) | You're designing a coordination protocol and need to understand how public announcements collapse epistemic divergence across the agent network |
+| [public-announcement-as-model-restriction.md](public-announcement-as-model-restriction.md) | You need the formal mechanism: how announcement φ replaces model M with restricted subgraph M^φ, eliminating worlds where ¬φ |
+| [satisfiability-as-autonomous-reconfiguration.md](satisfiability-as-autonomous-reconfiguration.md) | You want agents to autonomously find a configuration satisfying an epistemic specification, not merely verify an existing one |
+| [satisfiability-as-perceptual-planning.md](satisfiability-as-perceptual-planning.md) | You need to arrange sensors or agents so that a desired epistemic property φ becomes true — the inverse problem of model checking |
+| [two-phase-architecture-ontic-epistemic.md](two-phase-architecture-ontic-epistemic.md) | You're architecting a multi-agent system and need to cleanly separate world-changing actions (ontic) from knowledge-changing actions (epistemic) |
+| [vision-sets-and-perceptual-boundary-design.md](vision-sets-and-perceptual-boundary-design.md) | You're designing agent perceptual boundaries and need to understand which knowledge states are achievable given the agent's observational constraints |
